  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA weak cold front will move through the region today with the only \nnotable effects being a shift of winds from light southwesterly to \nlight northerly and some modest moisture pooling along the boundary \nas it's sliding south. Otherwise, well above normal temperatures are \nexpected today with highs nearing 15 degrees above normal in our \nsoutheastern zones. Temperatures will be slightly cooler, though \nstill much above normal, for Thursday as high pressure moves into \nthe Great Lakes region, allowing surface/ low-level flow to become \nsoutheasterly. Models are in decent agreement developing \nprecipitation along the nose of developing low-level jet\nThursday night. However, the strongest convergence and forcing \nis north of the area and we'll see little if any chances for \nprecipitation, even in far northern Missouri. \n\nFriday into Saturday, a shortwave trough digs into the Southern\nPlains and then moves into the Southeast. As it's doing this, \nit will force what's left of current Tropical Depression 9, away\nfrom the local area with little or no impact on our weather. \nAll of this will occur within a broader scale upper ridge \nmigrating across North America, keeping the temperatures much \nabove normal through the weekend and into the first part of next\nweek. It's not until the Tuesday - Wednesday time frame that \nmeaningful precipitation chances return to the area, when a weak\nboundary may slide south into the area. With modest moisture \nadvection into that boundary, there's at least a chance for \nprecipitation. \n\nLooking at the middle to later portions of next week, it\ncurrently looks more active. Ensemble 500mb height clusters are \nshowing a mix of a deepening trough over the Southwest and more \nsubtle/weaker troughing developing further south. By Wednesday, \nthis becomes even less distinct as the guidance spread \nincreases. But a deepening trough in the Southwest would be a \nmore favorable pattern for our area for precipitation, as it \nwould tend to draw moisture northward and eject embedded \nshortwave into the Plains. There is considerable uncertainty \nwith this still so things will change inevitably between now and\nnext week.",
